THE PULL IS CONCENTRATED: co2_level_SPO + co2_level_MLO + d14c_SH carry +310 of the +324 log-units
(96 %) between h_ml = 100 and 32.
AND IT IS OPPOSED, by d13c_level_MLO + d13c_level_SPO + gcb_ocean_sink (-56 together) -- the d13C LEVELS and the
ocean sink want a DEEP mixed layer. So h_ml is not being pulled by consensus;
it is being pulled by the CO2 LEVEL record against the d13C level record.

THE READING, AND IT DISCOUNTS PART 6's 236. A thinner mixed layer takes up
less carbon, so airborne CO2 rises: the CO2-level streams want h_ml small
because the model at init OVER-ABSORBS. Look at their absolute size -- SPO is
-256 log-units at h_ml = 100. Init is a BAD FIT to the CO2 record, and h_ml is
simply the cheapest single knob that patches it while the other 19 are frozen.
kz, k_w and beta_max all move ocean and land uptake directly, i.e. they serve
EXACTLY the three streams carrying 96 % of the pull.

SO: part 6's 236 log-units is an upper bound AND probably a large
overstatement of what bounding h_ml would really cost a refit. What survives
unconditionally is the NEGATIVE result -- the three gradient rows are
irrelevant to where h_ml goes (deleting them moves the argmax 32 -> 32 m), so
no re-formulation of them is a fix for the pull. Whether the pull itself
survives a refit is the refit's question and this file cannot answer it.

